  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12b-2002-p12.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Set \\(\\dfrac{n}{20-n}=k^2.\\) Solving, \\(n=\\dfrac{20k^2}{k^2+1}.\\) Since \\(k^2\\) and \\(k^2+1\\) are coprime, \\(k^2+1\\) must divide \\(20,\\) which happens only for \\(|k|=0,1,2,3.\\)\n\nThe signs of \\(k\\) do not change \\(n;\\) these give \\(n=0,\\) \\(10,\\) \\(16,\\) and \\(18,\\) which is four values.\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Set \\(\\dfrac{n}{20-n}=k^2\\) and solve for \\(n\\) in terms of \\(k\\)",
        "\\(n=\\dfrac{20k^2}{k^2+1}\\); since \\(\\gcd(k^2,k^2+1)=1,\\) \\(k^2+1\\) must divide \\(20\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
